WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.197
IVF: search only the nearby buckets.

2
00:00:02.477 --> 00:00:04.717
The second family partitions the space.

3
00:00:04.997 --> 00:00:18.267
An inverted file (IVF) index runs k-means on a sample of the vectors to find nlist centroids, then assigns each vector to its nearest centroid, so every centroid owns a bucket (an “inverted list”).

4
00:00:18.547 --> 00:00:25.032
At query time the index finds the nprobe centroids closest to the query and scans only those buckets.

5
00:00:25.312 --> 00:00:26.741
The trade-off is direct.

6
00:00:27.021 --> 00:00:34.637
With nprobe of 1, you scan about 1/nlist of the data and miss true neighbors that sit just across a bucket boundary.

7
00:00:34.917 --> 00:00:43.024
Raising nprobe scans more buckets, raising recall and cost together; with nprobe equal to nlist you are back to exact search.

8
00:00:43.304 --> 00:00:58.387
Faiss’s guidance suggests nlist on the order of the square root of n, a few times over (its wiki gives a rule of thumb near 10 times the square root, and its index guide suggests 4 to 16 times for collections under a million vectors).

9
00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:09.760
Unlike HNSW, IVF needs a training step to learn centroids, and if your data drifts far from the training sample, the buckets become unbalanced and recall can fall.

10
00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:17.912
pgvector’s IVFFlat index exposes the same dial, called probes, and defaults it to 1, which favors speed.

11
00:01:18.192 --> 00:01:24.165
You’ll build a small IVF index in the exercise and watch recall climb as nprobe grows.
